        },
        {
          "metadata": {
            "name": "Generate ActionItems"
          },
          "outcomes": {
            "success": "end"
          },
          "code": "GEN_ACTION_ITEMS",
          "id": "1770380929676",
          "inputs": [
            {
              "id": "1770380929677_1",
              "name": "prompt",
              "type": "string",
              "value": "##Context\nYou are an AI Assistant to {{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_name}}, {{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_designation}} at {{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_company}}.\n\nReturn only pure RFC 8259 compliant JSON using exactly the structure in ##Sample. Do not return any extra text, comments, or markup.\n\n##Instructions\n- Generate action items from ##References.\n- The email thread is in reverse chronological order, with the latest email first.\n- Use the latest email as the primary source for action items.\n- Use earlier emails only when the latest email clearly continues, repeats, or keeps an earlier action item open.\n- My email id is {{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_email}}.\n- Return at most 5 action items.\n- Each action item must represent exactly one independent action.\n- Exclude duplicates already logged in ##Past Activities within the last 72 hours when both the action type and primary business intent are the same.\n\n##Rules\n1. Create action items only from explicit requests, explicit commitments, or explicit meeting asks in the thread.\n2. Do not create action items from background context, general status updates, customer intent, or inferred follow-up.\n3. Valid action item types are only \"task\" and \"meeting\".\n4. Use \"task\" for explicit requests for information, documents, deliverables, confirmation, or another concrete follow-up.\n5. Use \"meeting\" for explicit requests to meet, call, schedule, discuss, review, or connect.\n6. If there are no action items, return exactly: {\"action_items\": []}\n7. Use null for any field that is not explicitly available or not clearly supported by the thread.\n8. Set \"owner\" to my email when the action is directed to me, requested from me, or I am the clear responsible party based on the thread. If another responsible person is clearly named and their email is explicitly present in the thread, use that email. Otherwise set \"owner\" to null.\n9. \"action_item\" must be concise, contain one clear action, and contain no date, time, timezone, or day name. If I am not the owner and another responsible person is clearly named, start \"action_item\" with that person?s name.\n10. Use due_date when the thread provides an exact date, a relative date that can be unambiguously resolved from the email timestamp or thread context, or a clearly implied deadline such as ?by EOD tomorrow,? ?early next week,? or ?within 3 business days.? Convert the resolved date to DD-MMM-YYYY. If the date cannot be determined with high confidence, use null.\n11. Use exactly these keys for every action item: \"action_item\", \"reasoning\", \"owner\", \"due_date\", \"type\", \"subject\", \"agenda\". Do not add any other keys.\n12. \"reasoning\" must be one concise sentence based only on explicit evidence from the thread.\n13. For \"task\", set \"subject\" to null and \"agenda\" to null. For \"meeting\", set \"subject\" to a clear meeting title and for \"agenda\", write a customer-facing appointment description in a natural email style. It must:\n\ta.\tbegin with a brief greeting and opening line,\n\tb.\tinclude the name or names of the meeting participants when they can be inferred from the email thread,\n\tc.\texplain the purpose of the meeting in one short sentence,\n\td.\tlist the main topics as bullet points,\n\te.\tend with a short closing line such as ?Looking forward to speaking with you? or similar,\n\tf.\tstay grounded only in the thread and never invent topics, names, or outcomes.\n        g.     you can refer to ##Agenda format example for reference.\n\n##Agenda format example:\n\nHi Thomas & Charlie, \n\nSetting up this time to connect and walk through the next steps for the Hybrid Generators order. During the call, we will cover:\n\n- Required procurement documentation\n- Order details for the 18 Hybrid Generators\n- Delivery timeline and scheduling\n- Any open questions or clarifications\n\nLooking forward to speaking with you.\n\n##Sample\n{\n  \"action_items\": [\n    {\n      \"action_item\": \"Send required procurement documentation to Thomas Crain\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thomas Crain asked for the documentation needed to move forward with procurement.\",\n      \"owner\": \"{{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_email}}\",\n      \"due_date\": \"11-APR-2026\",\n      \"type\": \"task\",\n      \"subject\": null,\n      \"agenda\": null\n    },\n    {\n      \"action_item\": \"Schedule procurement review meeting with Thomas Crain\",\n      \"reasoning\": \"Thomas Crain requested a discussion on procurement next steps.\",\n      \"owner\": \"{{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.person_email}}\",\n      \"due_date\": \"13-APR-2026\",\n      \"type\": \"meeting\",\n      \"subject\": \"Procurement Review for Server Expansion\",\n      \"agenda\": \"This meeting is intended to review the procurement steps required to move the Green Servers order forward, confirm the relevant documentation and order details, address any open questions, and align on the next steps and responsibilities for both teams.\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\n##References\n{{$context.$nodes.PARSE_EMAIL_DATA.$output.result.emailText}}\n\n##Past Activities\n{{$context.$nodes.QUERY_RECENT_ACTIVITIES.$output}}"
            }
          ],
          "type": "LLM"
        }
      ],
